          "thinking": "I need the yaṅluk form of viś, built on the reduplicated stem vevis-/veveś-; yaṅluganta forms take parasmaipada only. For the luṅ (aorist), since viś is aniṭ and falls under the śal-anta igupadha anit rule, I'm working out whether it takes the kṣa-aorist pattern.\n\nBut since the yaṅluk stem vevis is no longer a single-vowel (ekāc) root, the rule that makes it aniṭ (7.2.10) doesn't apply here, so the root becomes seṭ in this derivation instead. Comparing with bhū's yaṅluk luṅ pattern (abobhūvīt/abobhot), I'm reasoning that viś would similarly take sic with iṭ augment, giving forms like aveveśīt for third person singular.\n\nI'm working out whether laghūpadha guṇa (7.3.86) applies to the veveś- stem, checking it against the seṭ/aniṭ vṛddhi rules (7.2.3, 7.2.4, 7.2.7) that govern halanta roots in sic parasmaipada — concluding guṇa rather than vṛddhi applies here since the augment is iṭ.\n\nNow I'm checking whether 1.1.4 (na dhātulopa ārdhadhātuke) blocks guṇa in the yaṅluk formation — the question is whether the luk-deletion of yaṅ counts as a dhātu-part deletion caused by an ārdhadhātuka affix, or whether it's governed instead by 2.4.74, in which case the prohibition wouldn't apply.\n\nI recall the Kāśikā examples under 1.1.4 — \"lolupaḥ, popuvaḥ, marīmṛjaḥ, sarīsṛpaḥ\" — where yaṅ-luk occurs before an ac pratyaya, and since that lopa is caused by an ārdhadhātuka affix, guṇa gets blocked. But the general yaṅluk cases covered by \"carkarītaṃ ca\" allow more flexibility through the bahulam of \"yaṅo'ci ca.\"\n\nTrying to recall the Siddhānta Kaumudī forms for yaṅluk of bhū — bobhavāñcakāra, bobhavitā, bobhaviṣyati, bobhūtu, abobhavīt, bobhūyāt, abobhūvīt, abobhaviṣyat — noting that guṇa does appear in several of these forms.\n\nApplying that pattern to viś yaṅluk, I'd expect luṅ forms like aveveśīt, aveveśiṣṭām, aveveśiṣuḥ, and the rest of the paradigm with iṭ-āgama throughout — though I need to double-check whether iṭ is actually established for